From cf047c295cc5fc74320e9719ada455e61e157441 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Fri, 20 Nov 2026 14:32:33 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 1/2] Fault in a static binding's fresh pages in a few calls
 (#2546)

A static hello world linked by the C driver in a fresh process spends its
`executable` stage (the binding and link_check) on pages it touches for
the first time. In a cloud VM, whose arenas are base pages, the stage took
319 page faults (perf record -e page-faults -d, by address): 314 were the
binding's own writes, 1,256 KB of fresh anonymous pages (171 for the
members' atoms' bytes in the program's byte arena, 123 for the link's
columns and names past their counts, 20 for Bind's arrays), and 3 mapped
6 MB of the snapshot's 2 MB folios, most of the 7.3 MB the stage added to
the resident set. With the snapshot on tmpfs (base pages) and the arenas
in huge pages, as on the 9700X, the stage took the issue's numbers, 100
faults and 6.1 MB: 63 fault-arounds of the snapshot, 16 for the byte
arena (one of them its huge page) and 19 other base pages.

bind_populate, once the static closure knows the members, faults in what
their joins are about to write, a call per arena or column
(MADV_POPULATE_WRITE): the members' atoms' rooms in the byte arena
(lane.c's arena_populate, with arena_fault_in), their names' bytes, and
each column's records past its count (link.c's link_populate). It does so
only where the byte arena hasn't held that much before, so that a link
bound again, as the benchmarks bind one, doesn't walk pages it has. A
fresh base page costs about 1.7 us as a fault in the VM and 1.0 us
populated among others.

In the VM (Develop builds, medians of 61 interleaved fresh processes of
`-static hello.c`) the stage took 1.20 ms against 1.37, with 25 faults
taken for 319, and the whole compile 1,300 for 1,585 (perf stat). With
huge pages and the snapshot on tmpfs it is unchanged (1.20 against
1.18 ms). The executables are byte-identical: the static and dynamic hello
worlds, and Lua 5.4.8 linked -static from its 33 files.

Populating the snapshot doesn't pay: MADV_POPULATE_READ over the 704
pages a link reads, in 76 ranges, took about what the faults took, and
over the whole span more. What remains of a fresh link is reading: the
pieces' hashes' first reads of 11,206 slices, the joins' copies and
link_check. docs/object-writer-and-linker.md ("The runtime snapshot") has
the measurements.

test-lanes checks arena_fault_in and arena_populate, test-link
link_populate's every column, and test-bind bind_populate and that bind_end
populates (mincore and the arenas' peaks); each of 49 mutants of the new
code fails one of them.

