+**A link in a fresh process** (#2546). A compile loads the snapshot and binds
+once, where the measurements above bind again and again in one process. In a
+cloud VM (4 vCPUs, the cache on ext4, the arenas in base pages as on every
+virtual machine: `arena_page_policy`), `-static hello.c`'s `executable` stage
+(`--time-stages`: the binding and `link_check`) took 319 page faults and
+7.3 MB more resident, where a dynamic hello world's took 3. By address
+(`perf record -e page-faults -d`):
+
+- 314 were the binding's writes to fresh anonymous pages, 1,256 KB: 171 for
+  the members' atoms' bytes in the program's byte arena, 123 for the link's
+  columns and names past their counts, 20 for `Bind`'s arrays;
+- 3 were the snapshot's, mapping 6 MB of its 2 MB folios: the link reads
+  1,229 KB of it, 11,206 slices of 429 pieces over 704 pages.
+
+With the snapshot in base pages (on tmpfs) and the arenas in huge pages, as
+on the 9700X, the stage took 100 faults and 6.1 MB, the issue's 103 and
+6.5 MB: 63 for the snapshot, each a fault-around of 64 KB (4 MB in all), 16
+for the byte arena (the rest of its 64 KB of base pages and one huge page)
+and 19 for the other arenas' base pages.
+
+A fresh base page cost about 1.7 µs as a fault in the VM, and 1.0 µs when
+`MADV_POPULATE_WRITE` faults it in among others in one call (328 pages: 515
+to 594 µs touched, 308 to 347 populated). So binding faults in what the
+members' joins are about to write (`bind_populate`) once the closure knows
+the members: their atoms' rooms in the byte arena (`arena_populate`), their
+names' bytes, and each column's records past its count (`link_populate`), a
+call each, where the byte arena hasn't held that much before. A link bound
+again keeps its pages, which the advice would only walk (about 30 µs for
+1.3 MB of them in 32 calls in the VM). In the VM the stage took 1.20 ms
+against 1.37 (medians of 61 interleaved runs), with 25 faults taken for 319
+(`perf`'s `page-faults`; getrusage, which `--time-stages` reads, counts the
+populated pages too: 335), and the whole compile 1,300 for 1,585. With huge
+pages and the snapshot in base pages it is unchanged (1.20 ms against 1.18),
+as it should be on the 9700X.
+
+Populating the snapshot doesn't pay: `MADV_POPULATE_READ` over the 704
+pages a link reads, in 76 ranges, took about what the faults took (medians
+of 7 processes: 242 µs against 206 on tmpfs; of 5: 46 against 22 on ext4's
+large folios), and over the whole span more (398 µs on tmpfs). What remains
+of a fresh link is reading: the pieces' hashes, the first reads of their
+11,206 slices, the joins' copies and `link_check` (0.26 ms of the VM's
+stage, which `benchmark-link`'s link leaves out), in that order of the
+stage's samples (`perf`'s `cpu-clock`).
+
